c(49, "§4.4; §4.5; §4.6", "insight", "3★ (4) is a considered verdict — the richest negative review praises the graphs then lists no achievement feeling, a stale date, no monthly missed-day view, 'lacks the punch' and the only stated churn ('Uninstalling it after buying the premium version and using it for 20 days'); the Korean 3★ is the most detailed review of any rating. 2★ (3) are structural: a lifetime purchaser whose app crashes on every launch and whose Health connection fails ('the highest-value complaint in the corpus'); 'no possibility to get a lifetime version'; 'Nur ein Streak ist free' — two of three about the shape of the purchase. 1★ (6): 5 of 6 monetization-only; only one names a defect; all in a 70-day window; five of six from storefronts contributing exactly one review (dk, pk, pt, pl, ca).", "n/a", "complaint", "3★ 4; 2★ 3; 1★ 6", "none", "table", "generalisable", ["12339835181", "12579762746", "14209508686", "14359876847", "12552768633", "12652787691", "14355567891"], "", "")

c(50, "§4.7 table (verbatim); §4.7", "data-caveat", "Rating does not fix theme direction (verbatim): " + table("## 4.7 Rating does not fix", 0) + " — 60 of 71 (84.51%) contain praise including 1 of 6 one-star and 1 of 3 two-star; a 5★ reports a crash; money-negative spans the whole scale.", "n/a", "mixed", "praise_any at every rating; money_negative 3/2/2/2/5 across 5★→1★", "none", "table", "generalisable", ["13063341493", "13064284382"], "", "")

c(51, "§5.1; §5.2 table (verbatim); §5.2", "data-caveat", "Every piece of direct purchase evidence (verbatim): " + table("## 5.2 Every piece of direct purchase", 0) + " — four of 71 (5.63%, CI 2.2–13.6%) state they paid, one implies it; zero refund / cancellation / renewal; no conversion, renewal, refund or churn rate is derivable; mean rating of the four stated payers 3.75★ (0.56 below the corpus): two 5★ satisfied, two unhappy about the product rather than the price — a lifetime buyer whose app crashes on launch ('maximum spend, zero delivery', the worst outcome in the corpus) and a 20-day churner who left over missing feedback loops.", "n/a", "mixed", "PAID_STATED 4 / 3.75★; lifetime SKU existed 2025-04-17; subscriber 5★; in-app content buyer 5★", "must-never-break", "High-priority", "generalisable", ["12339835181", "12552768633", "13200826254", "13809152355", "14331668442"], "", "")

c(52, "§5.3", "insight", "What causes people to consider paying — direct evidence one review: the concept ('more useful for me than more traditional habit trackers') and the purchase model ('The one time purchase rather than a subscription model is also a lovely thing'), blocked by affordability ('the moment I can afford it'); inferred from the three who volunteer the price is right: comparative value against other trackers already paid for or tried ('really affordable compared to others'; 'the price is just right'; two of three also COMP_TRIED_MANY) — 'this app converts against a reference price the user has already formed elsewhere, not against zero' (inference, n=3); no reviewer names a specific feature as the thing they paid for; the nearest is buying forest tiles (cosmetic content).", "one-time purchase praised; comparative value", "purchase-driver", "MONP_INTENT 1; MONP_VALUE 3 / 5.00★; MONP_AFFORD_BARRIER 1", "build-paid", "limited evidence", "generalisable", ["13941527190", "12905347299", "13019604470", "13410412793", "14331668442"], "", "")

c(53, "§5.4 table (verbatim); §5.4", "monetization", "Upgrade barriers in order of evidence (verbatim): " + table("## 5.4 Upgrade barriers", 0) + " — 'the ordering is the finding': structure and disclosure sit above price on every measure — more reviews, lower ratings, external corroboration; price level is the smallest of the seven; affordability (wants to buy, cannot yet) is a 5★.", "n/a", "blocked-conversion", "wall 7 / 2.86★; cap 4 / 2.50★; undisclosed 2 / 1.50★; confusion 3 / 3.00★; no trial 1 / 4.00★; nag 1 / 1.00★; price 2 / 3.00★; affordability 1 / 5.00★", "product-rule", "High-priority", "generalisable", ["13307114320", "13349206242", "14355567891", "12652787691", "12530006457", "13200826254", "13941527190"], "", "")

c(54, "§5.5; §8.4", "monetization", "The one-habit cap as a conversion instrument: an aggressive but coherent strategy — it makes the core loop un-experienceable without paying, because a habit tracker with one habit is a demo; the cost side is precise (four who hit it rate 2.50★; 'you can't really explore the app'; add one habit then be interrupted repeatedly) but the corpus cannot say whether the cap works — no conversion data; four unhappy reviewers visible, any number of quiet converters not; the report does not recommend removing the cap, it recommends disclosing it.", "1-habit cap", "blocked-conversion", "cap_any 4 / 2.50★; conversion unknown", "research", "High-priority", "generalisable", ["13349206242", "12530006457", "14080954809"], "", "the cap's conversion effect is unmeasured; only its complaint cost is visible")

c(55, "§5.6", "data-caveat", "Refund, cancellation and renewal — an explicit blank: searched in six languages (refund, erstattung, reembolso, money back, 환불, cancel, kündig, cancelar, 해지) — zero hits; one churn event (uninstall, not refund); 'a genuine blank in the evidence, not a clean bill of health' — App Store reviews under-represent post-purchase experience because the prompt fires early; read as unobserved, not absent.", "n/a", "mixed", "0 refund; 0 cancel; 1 churn", "research", "absence test", "generalisable", ["12339835181"], "", "")

